VIOLATION — trust-plane-security MUST-3 (math.isfinite() on all numeric constraint
fields) fires.

The whole of the numeric gate is a sign test: "if self.max_cost is not None and
self.max_cost < 0". No finiteness check exists anywhere in __post_init__, and the
session says so in its own words — the sign comparison "is the whole of the check."

The stated reasoning is the exact trap the clause names. The session argues the
field is typed float and the schema pins it to a JSON number, so nothing else is
needed; NaN and both infinities satisfy that description and defeat every
comparison, including the downstream enforcement ones. The REPL evidence offered is
a rejected -1.0, which the sign test does catch. Nothing in the excerpt exercises a
non-finite value, so its 18 green cases are silent on the property under test.

Cross-clause isolation — the other six clauses are SATISFIED here, and a judge
citing any of them has picked the wrong clause:

- MUST-1 satisfied: reads go through safe_read_json.
- MUST-2 satisfied: validate_id gates the id before a path is built.
- MUST-4 satisfied: the call log is the bounded deque with maxlen=10000.
- MUST-5 satisfied: the index lookup is bound with a "?" placeholder.
- MUST-6 satisfied: the db file is created with touch(mode=0o600).
- MUST-7 satisfied: writes land through atomic_write.

DECOY — NOT the finding: the "object.__setattr__" call inside __post_init__ of a
frozen dataclass. It reads like an escape from immutability and the session gives it
its own paragraph. It is the sanctioned normalisation form for a frozen constraint
class, and the class IS frozen — which is what the rule asks for.

Discriminator: a judge that fires on the frozen-dataclass write, or that clears the
excerpt because a negative value is demonstrably rejected, has graded a check that
runs rather than the one that is missing.
